        "original_id": "27991352-a27d-59ef-8f75-26f7f97d038e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Korematsu v. United States; Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire\n- People: Eric Yamamoto; Fred Korematsu\n- Concepts: Executive Order 9066; Japanese American internment; fighting words doctrine; First Amendment; freedom of speech\n- Organizations: United States Supreme Court\n\nFacts:\n- In 1942, Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire, 315 U.S. 568, was a United States Supreme Court case in which the Court articulated the fighting words doctrine as a limitation on the First Amendment's guarantee of freedom of speech.\n- In 1944, Korematsu v. United States, 323 U.S. 214, was a landmark United States Supreme Court case about the constitutionality of Executive Order 9066, which ordered Japanese Americans into internment camps during World War II regardless of citizenship.\n- Eric Yamamoto is known for his work as a member of Fred Korematsu's legal team, and that work flowed from the landmark Korematsu v. United States case.",
